    "completenessNote": "补译缺口条文；条号与原文一一对应。",
    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "1. Any person commits an offence if that person unlawfully and intentionally: a. seizes or exercises control over a fixed platform by force or threat thereof or any other form of intimidation; or\nb. performs an act of violence against a person on board a fixed platform if that act is likely to endanger its safety; or c. destroys a fixed platform or causes damage to it in a manner likely to endanger its safety; or d. places or causes to be placed on a fixed platform, by any means whatsoever, a device or substance which is likely to destroy that fixed platform or to endanger its safety.\ne. injures or kills any person, in connection with the commission or the attempted commission of any of the offences set forth in subparagraphs (a) to (d).\n2. Any person also commits an offence if that person: a. attempts to commit any of the offences set forth in paragraph 1; or b. abets the commission of any of the offences set forth in paragraph 1 by any other person, participates as an accomplice in the commission of such an offence, or otherwise participates in it; or c. threatens, with or without a condition, as provided for under national law, to commit an offence set forth in paragraph 1, subparagraph (b) or (c), where that threat is likely to endanger the safety of the fixed platform. Article 3\n1. Each State Party shall take such measures as may be necessary to establish its jurisdiction over the offences set forth in Article 2 when the offence is committed: a. against or on board a fixed platform while it is located on the continental shelf of that State; or b. by a national of that State. 2. A State Party may also establish its jurisdiction over any such offence when: a. it is committed by a stateless person who has his or her habitual residence in that State; or b. during the commission of the offence a national of that State is seized, threatened, injured or killed; or c. the offence is committed in an attempt to compel that State to do or abstain from doing any act.\n3. A State Party which has established jurisdiction in respect of the cases referred to in paragraph 2 shall notify the Secretary-General of the International Maritime Organization (hereinafter referred to as “the Secretary-General”). If that State Party subsequently withdraws such jurisdiction, it shall notify the Secretary-General.\n4. Each State Party shall take such measures as may be necessary to establish its jurisdiction over the offences set forth in Article 2 in cases where the alleged offender is present in its territory and it does not extradite that person to any of the States Parties which have established their jurisdiction in accordance with paragraph 1 or 2 of this Article.\n5. This Protocol does not exclude any criminal jurisdiction exercised in accordance with national law.\nNothing in this Protocol shall affect the rules of international law relating to fixed platforms located on the continental shelf."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "1. This Protocol shall be open for signature at Rome on 10 March 1988 and at the Headquarters of the International Maritime Organization (hereinafter referred to as “the Organization”) from 14 March 1988 to 09 March 1989 by any State which has signed the Convention.\nThereafter, this Protocol shall remain open for accession.\n2. States may express their consent to be bound by this Protocol by: a. signature without reservation as to ratification, acceptance or approval; or b. signature subject to ratification, acceptance or approval, followed by ratification, acceptance or approval; or c. accession.\n3. Ratification, acceptance, approval or accession shall be effected by the deposit of an instrument to that effect with the Secretary-General. 4. Only a State which has signed the Convention without reservation as to ratification, acceptance or approval, or has deposited an instrument of ratification, acceptance, approval or accession, or has acceded to the Convention, may become a Party to this Protocol."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5"
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "1. This Protocol shall enter into force 90 days after three States have signed it without reservation as to ratification, acceptance or approval, or have deposited an instrument of ratification, acceptance, approval or accession. However, this Protocol shall not enter into force before the Convention has entered into force. 2. For a State which deposits an instrument of ratification, acceptance, approval or accession after the conditions for entry into force have been fulfilled, that ratification, acceptance, approval or accession shall take effect 90 days after the date of such deposit.\n[Illegible in source]\n1. This Protocol may be denounced by any State Party at any time after the expiry of one year from the date on which this Protocol enters into force for that State.\n2. Denunciation shall be effected by the deposit of an instrument of denunciation with the Secretary-General.\n3. A denunciation shall take effect one year, or such longer period as may be specified in the instrument of denunciation, after its receipt by the Secretary-General.\n4. A denunciation of the Convention by a State Party shall be deemed to be a denunciation of this Protocol by that Party.\n1. A conference may be convened by the Organization for the purpose of revising or amending this Protocol. 2. The Secretary-General may convene a conference of the States Parties to this Protocol to consider a revision or amendment at the request of one third of the States Parties, or of five States Parties, whichever is the higher number.\n3. Any instrument of ratification, acceptance, approval or accession deposited after the date of entry into force of an amendment to this Protocol shall be deemed to apply to this Protocol as amended.\n1. This Protocol shall be deposited with the Secretary-General.\n2. The Secretary-General shall:\na. inform all States which have signed this Protocol or acceded to it, and all members of the Organization, of: (1) each new signature or deposit of an instrument of ratification, acceptance, approval or accession, together with the date thereof;\n(2) the date of entry into force of this Protocol;\n(3) the deposit of any instrument of denunciation of this Protocol, together with the date on which it was received and the date on which the denunciation takes effect;\n(4) the receipt of any declaration or notification made under this Protocol or the Convention relating to this Protocol;\nb. transmit certified true copies of this Protocol to all States which have signed or acceded to this Protocol.\n3. As soon as this Protocol enters into force, a certified true copy thereof shall be transmitted by the depositary to the Secretary-General of the United Nations for registration and publication in accordance with Article 102 of the Charter of the United Nations.\nArticle 10 This Protocol is drawn up in a single original in the Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish languages, each text being equally authentic.\nIn witness whereof the undersigned, being duly authorised by their respective Governments for that purpose, have signed this Protocol.\nDone at Rome on 10 March 1988."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6"
        }
      }
    ]
